## Feature folder structure

Each user-facing surface gets its own folder under `lib/src/<feature>/` with a **flat** layout:

```
lib/src/<feature>/
  <feature>_page.dart      # entry route widget — class *Page
  components/              # feature-local UI building blocks only
  models/                  # optional — only if UI needs a dedicated mapped shape
```

Real examples in this project:
- `lib/src/auth/signin_page.dart` — entry page, widget `SigninPage`
- `lib/src/posts/components/` — post-specific UI pieces
- `lib/src/account/components/` — account-specific UI pieces

### Forbidden under `lib/src/<feature>/`
- `presentation/` subfolder
- `screens/` subfolder
- `viewmodels/` subfolder
- `widgets/` folder at the feature level — use `components/` instead
- Provider files — providers live in `lib/data/providers/` only

### Shared widgets
Reusable widgets that span features belong in `lib/utils/widgets/`, not inside any feature folder.

## Widget behaviour

### Pages (`*_page.dart`)
- Are **entry points only** — they wire the UI to Riverpod state.
- Read state with `ref.watch`; trigger actions with `ref.read(...notifier)`.
- Contain no business logic, no API calls, no state mutation.
- Do not call repositories or Retrofit clients.

### Components (`components/`)
- Are **dumb and presentational** — receive data via constructor, emit events via callbacks.
- No `ref`, no provider reads unless genuinely needed for very local UI state.
- No API calls, no side effects.

## State (ViewModel layer)
All business logic and async orchestration live in Riverpod codegen providers under `lib/data/providers/`. Do not add a `viewmodels/` folder under any feature.

## Naming
- File: `snake_case`, suffix `_page.dart` for route entry widgets.
- Widget class: `PascalCase`, suffix `Page` (e.g. `SigninPage`, `PostsPage`).
- Components: descriptive names reflecting their visual role, not their parent page.
